#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078139 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5414e634-9b33-5dac-9686-4e570efbc7e6" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing an Exponential Function and a Polynomial Function"}] :question/id #uuid "ce4b3604-5402-5cff-9ff4-fa73b6af3885" :question/math-academy-id "e-237" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{x^{100}}{e^x}$." :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty$. However, since $e^x \\gg x^{100}$ for large values of $x$, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{x^{100}}{e^x} = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078140 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "433ac237-32ff-591c-ab0d-e271f5beda30" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing a Polynomial Function and a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "811da414-bf42-50dd-9521-3174fbfc8340" :question/math-academy-id "e-238" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{x}{\\ln{x}}$." :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty$. However, since $x \\gg \\ln{x}$ for large values of $x$, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{x}{\\ln{x}} = \\infty\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078141 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c538b0a9-a084-5a83-a5fa-eca4e0270a93" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing an Exponential Function and a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "39e8332d-2c2f-5f03-b97d-6be35b31db85" :question/math-academy-id "e-239" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{e^{2x}}{\\ln{x}}$." :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty$. However, since $e^{2x} \\gg \\ln{x}$ for large values of $x$, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{e^{2x}}{\\ln{x}} = \\infty\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078142 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "579373bc-38b3-584c-a118-84be7dfc9074" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing the Relative Magnitude of a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f33d2107-befc-53cf-b835-c9adb731b707" :question/math-academy-id "e-241" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2+1}$." :question/worked-solution "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n\\mid \\cos x \\mid \\leq 1\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $x^2+1$ grows without bound as $x\\to\\infty$. So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2+1} = 0\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2+1}\n$$\n\nshown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/607/Source/Images/2887328aab485652d020545444662790.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "433ac237-32ff-591c-ab0d-e271f5beda30" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing a Polynomial Function and a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86ab195b-9f8e-5e2a-b3b4-48f754809e77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076618} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec4a3a9-b508-5298-a687-73efc37172b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076619} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f38b75-7a15-5930-873f-b598ae096865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076620} {:answer/id #uuid "010c631a-aef8-5e37-b59b-5e3ac076d076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076621} {:answer/id #uuid "13147407-1